.TH MODBUS-SCRAPER 1 "2026-11-05" "pv-suite 0.4.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
modbus-scraper \- Prometheus exporter for PV inverters (Modbus TCP,
stdlib only)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modbus-scraper
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Prometheus exporter for PV inverters (Modbus TCP, stdlib
only). Polls the inverter over Modbus TCP and serves the huawei_* metrics
the dashboard expects at /metrics in the Prometheus text format: the PV
generation, the grid exchange (import/export split), the home consumption
implied by the balance, the battery and the health gauges
(scraper_up, the error counter, the last poll age).
.PP
Every poll is sanity-checked: a negative PV reading, an array larger than
\fB--max-kwp\fR, a grid line beyond \fB--max-grid-kw\fR or a negative
home balance marks the exposition \fB503\fR (the sensors are suspect, not
the poller) and names the register in the log. A modbus-cache in front of
the device is detected automatically (its synthesized diagnostics block)
and adds the device-up / cache-age / cache-uptime gauges.
.PP
Modbus auth (\fB--auth\fR): with \fBsuite\fR the session proves the
shared key (\fB--auth-key\fR, min 16 characters) with a crypt handshake
right after every connect \(em 52 bytes: magic, fresh nonce,
HMAC\-SHA256 proof; the peer answers \fBOK\fR and plain Modbus follows,
or \fBERR\fR and the connection fails (the poller keeps retrying).
Empty auth = no auth at all.
.PP
The exposition endpoint is the tool's serving surface: the connection caps
(\fB--max-clients\fR total, \fB--max-conn-per-ip\fR per peer, extras
get an immediate 503), the idle keep-alive kick (\fB--client-timeout\fR)
and the bearer token (\fB--auth-token\fR, the value a token or a
\fBfile:<path>\fR reference). A
non-loopback listen without a token refuses to start (F13 in
docs/AUDIT.md).
.PP
The JSON-lines control server (\fB--control-port\fR, talked to with
\fBmodbus-scraper-ctl\fR(1) and \fBmodbus-scraper-cli\fR(1)) inspects
and steers the running exporter: the live settings, an immediate poll, the
config reload, the Modbus session restart, the graceful shutdown. With a
control token configured the wire is HMAC-signed: every command and reply
carries the full sha256 of its payload, keyed by the token
(common/wiresec.py) \(em a token-holding client verifies both directions,
and over TCP the signed commands are mandatory. A second, read-only token
(\fB--control-ops-token\fR) authenticates into the show/clear verbs only
\(em the ops shift against the admin key, both roles audited.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-upstream
the inverter Modbus TCP endpoint HOST:PORT (`:5502` = 127.0.0.1:5502)
.TP
.BI \-\-modbus\-unit " N"
Modbus unit id
.TP
.BI \-\-modbus\-offset " N"
added to register addresses
.TP
.B \-\-auth
the inverter's Modbus auth scheme: `suite` proves the shared auth\-key
with a crypt handshake right after every connect (the peer answers OK,
or ERR and the connection fails — the poller keeps retrying); none/empty
= no auth at all
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-key
the shared key the suite handshake proves (min 16 characters); the value
may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a
systemd LoadCredential rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " N"
Modbus timeout in seconds
.TP
.B \-\-profile
device profile JSON (the shared profiles/ schema; scrape\-marked entries
feed the metric registers)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-spec
metrics spec JSON, sectioned: {inputs: {name: {help}}, metrics: {key:
{profile?, expr?, when?, help, type?}}, scraper:...} — the metrics
entries are register\- (profile) or expression\-fed (expr/when) over the
declared inputs (show inputs), merged in memory over the built\-in
lib/metrics.json scraper_* block
.TP
.B \-\-metric\-prefix
the prefix on the profile\- and compute\-fed metric names; the bundled
scraper_* names are fixed
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-kwp " N"
maximum PV array kWp
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-grid\-kw " N"
maximum grid kW, either direction
.TP
.B \-\-grid\-positive\-import
grid register positive when importing
.TP
.B \-\-listen
listen address HOST:PORT
.TP
.B \-\-local\-time
log timestamps in the system time zone instead of UTC
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP clients; extras get an immediate 503
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-timeout " N"
seconds an idle keep\-alive HTTP client is kept before being kicked
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-conn\-per\-ip " N"
maximum concurrent HTTP connections from one peer IP; extras get an
immediate 503
.TP
.B \-\-control\-port
JSON\-lines control server target: unix socket path or TCP port number
on 127.0.0.1 (e.g. 15599); 0 = off. Talk to it with
modbus\-scraper\-ctl.py
.TP
.B \-\-control\-token
require a token on control connections: the client authenticates with
{"cmd": "auth", "token":...}; over the unix socket a trusted peer (root,
the daemon's own user or \-\-control\-trust\-uids — SO_PEERCRED) skips
the token; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-token
a second, read\-only control token: sessions authenticating with it may
run the show/clear verbs only (no set/unset, no poll now, no request
reload, no restart, no shutdown) — the ops shift against the admin key,
both audited; the value may be `file:<path>` — the first line of an
owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential rides as
file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-control\-ops\-verbs
the read\-only ops role's RBAC policy: the verbs its sessions may run,
comma\-separated
.TP
.B \-\-control\-trust\-uids
extra peer uids the unix control socket trusts without the token
(SO_PEERCRED — a local systemd unit speaks without the secret); root and
the daemon's own user are always trusted
.TP
.B \-\-control\-socket\-group
leave the unix control socket group\-accessible (chmod 0660 instead of
the plain 0600) ($CONTROL_SOCKET_GROUP)
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-token
require Authorization: Bearer <token> on all requests; the value may be
`file:<path>` — the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd
LoadCredential rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.B \-\-metrics\-acl
clients allowed to connect to /metrics: "any", "none" (refuse everyone),
comma\-separated IPs/prefixes like 10.0.0.7,10.48.0.0/16, or
"file:<path>" — the standalone ip\-prefix acl file (the gate object
{"type": "ip\-prefix", "rules": [{"type": "allow"|"deny", "prefix":
\&...}]}: the last matching rule decides, a client no rule decides
refuses; "any" = 0.0.0.0/0, the whole space)
.TP
.B \-\-audit\-log
SCR audit trail: a file path or \- for stdout (the docker/journald way);
the XDG state home (~/.local/state/modbus\-scraper/audit.log) when unset
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-max\-mb " N"
rotate the audit log at this size in MB, keeping 3 backups; 0 = no size
rotation
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-files " N"
rotated audit backups to keep
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audit\-log
disable the SCR audit trail
.TP
.BI \-\-interval " N"
seconds between polls
.TP
.B \-\-log\-access
log every HTTP request at INFO
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level
log level
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-path
the state dump: the live setting overrides (the poll plan, the caps, the
log surface — the tokens apart) as one JSON, written 0600 with an
integrity hash (a restore refuses a tampered, unsigned or
group\-writable dump); dumped on the request dump command, the graceful
shutdown and every \-\-dump\-interval seconds; loaded on start only with
\-\-restore\-on\-start, on demand with the request restore command
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-key
the key that seals the state dumps and the reload's state file: the
state rides encrypted (an HMAC\-SHA256 counter\-mode keystream,
authenticated — a restore refuses a wrong or missing key, and a
configured key refuses an unsealed dump); the reload re\-exec
re\-resolves the same key, so the state file reads on the other side; no
key = the dumps stay 0600 plaintext with the integrity hash a dump path
without a key warns at start and on set; the value may be `file:<path>`
— the first line of an owner\-only 0600 file (a systemd LoadCredential
rides as file:/run/credentials/...)
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-interval " N"
seconds between the automatic state dumps, 0 = on request (the request
dump command and the shutdown)
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-on\-start
load the state dump when the daemon starts (needs \-\-dump\-path) — the
live overrides ride again instead of a cold start; without it the dump
loads on demand
.TP
.B \-\-config
config file — the set grammar: every line a `set <name> <value>`,
comments start with # ($CONFIG, then scraper.conf next to this script,
then the subproject root)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
validate the configuration, print the effective settings and exit
(nothing is bound or connected)


.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
re-read the config file (and the auth token file) and apply what is live
(the same path as the control server's request reload-config); a broken
config keeps the previous settings.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
graceful shutdown.

.SH CONTROL
The control server speaks JSON lines ({"cmd": "..."} ->
{"status": "ok|fail|bye", "text": "..."}; TCP requires the token when
configured and refuses to start without one). Commands:
\fBset\fR/\fBunset\fR/\fBshow\fR/\fBhelp\fR (the settings: metrics
interval, modbus timeout, the metrics limits caps, caps max-pv/max-grid,
log-access, log-level, control socket-group (re-modes the live unix socket
0600/0660) \(em and the startup-only secrets: control token, the ops
token, metrics auth token \(em the wire refuses set/unset, the value
never rendering \(em \fBshow <secret>\fR refuses), \fBshow connections\fR
(the live HTTP clients), \fBshow version\fR, \fBshow knobs\fR
(the dictionary of the registry), \fBpoll now\fR,
\fBinspect\fR, \fBrequest reload-config\fR (the SIGHUP path),
\fBrequest reload\fR (the whole-daemon restart that keeps the sets: the
live overrides save to a state file beside the config, the process
re-execs, the fresh daemon re-applies them once), with a dump path
configured (\fB--dump-path\fR): \fBrequest dump\fR / \fBrequest
restore\fR (the overrides as one 0600 integrity-hashed JSON;
\fB--dump-key\fR seals it \(em the state rides encrypted, a restore
refuses a wrong or missing key; \fB--restore-on-start\fR loads it at
start, \fB--dump-interval\fR rewrites it on a timer),
\fBrequest restart\fR, \fBshutdown\fR,
\fBquit\fR. See
.BR modbus-scraper-ctl (1)
and
.BR modbus-scraper-cli (1).

.SH SECURITY
The scraper reads an open Modbus device (no auth, no TLS — the inverter's
network isolation is the boundary) and serves /metrics: bind loopback,
set the bearer token when it must serve beyond loopback (a
non-loopback token-less start is refused, F13), and keep the connection
caps on. The control surface is loopback-only (TCP) or a 0600 unix socket;
a TCP control port without a token refuses to start (F5); with a token the
wire is signed and the commands/replies verify end to end; a second,
read-only ops token (\fB--control-ops-token\fR) scopes its sessions to
the show/clear verbs. The audit trail (in the XDG state home when
\fB--audit-log\fR is unset, prefix SCR) records the control commands
with their roles, the auth failures, the reloads and the shutdowns.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CTL
control target for the daemon's clients when the flag is unset (flag
\fB--control-port\fR wins).
.TP
.B UPSTREAM ", " LISTEN ", " INTERVAL ", " AUTH_TOKEN ", " ...
every flag has an environment twin of the same bare name (no tool
prefix); the flag wins over the env, the env over the config script.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/modbus-scraper-<listen-port>.socket
the control socket used when \fB--control-port\fR is unset.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/modbus-scraper/audit.log
the audit trail when \fB--audit-log\fR is unset.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success (\fB--dry-run\fR: valid configuration).
.TP
.B 1
invalid configuration or a failed bind (reason on stderr).
.TP
.B 2
command-line misuse.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR modbus-scraper-ctl (1),
.BR modbus-scraper-cli (1),
.BR modbus-cache (1),
.BR modbus-fake-inverter (1)
.PP
scraper/README.md in the suite checkout \(em the full documentation.
